  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nTODAY & TONIGHT\nThe stratus and fog that developed rapidly around sunrise this \nmorning has finally dissipated for most of the area this \nafternoon. Had some initial concerns for high temperatures areas\naround the Iron Range where the stratus has lingered the \nlongest. Where the cloud lingered, temperatures were still in \nthe 30s as of noon, but as soon as the cloud dissipates, \ntemperatures jump quickly into the 40s. There is a more \npersistent patch of clouds around Grand Marais extending north \nto Isle Royale, which may be at least partially driven by lake \neffects, and will be more persistent today. Winds are also \npicking up, with gusts of 20 to 25 mph. Tonight, a weak and dry \ncold front will sweep across the region from northwest to \nsoutheast. This frontal passage will shift our winds to the west\nand northwest, remaining breezy overnight. Due to a lack of \ndeep moisture, no precipitation is expected with this front, \nthough the winds and extra cloud cover will keep low \ntemperatures tonight in the mid to upper 30s.\n\nSUNDAY \nSunday will feature another warmer than normal day, despite the\novernight cold front. Highs will once again reach the mid 40s \nto lower 50s. Breezy northwest winds will persist through much \nof the day. Along the North Shore there may be a few gusts to \nover 30 mph during the morning and early afternoon hours. While \nconditions will remain mostly dry, a quick passing disturbance \nto the north could trigger a few isolated sprinkles in the tip \nof the Arrowhead during the morning, but no measurable \nprecipitation is expected. Sunday night will be colder with \novernight lows dropping into the 20s to low 30s once the winds \ndrop off as a ridge of high pressure builds in.\n\nMONDAY and TUESDAY \nAnother shortwave will move across Canada during this time \nframe, leaving the area with warmer than normal temperatures and\ngenerally dry weather. Highs should climb into the mid 40s to \nmid 50s both days, with overnight lows ranging from the upper \n20s to mid 30s. A dry front pushes across the area on Monday, \nbut with the main forcing to our north and little moisture to \nwork with, I only expect some increased cloud cover. \n\nTUESDAY NIGHT through THURSDAY\nA potent upper level disturbance slides southeast across the \narea Tuesday night into Wednesday, ushering in a much colder \nairmass to the region. This will cause high temperatures to drop\ninto the 40s for Wednesday and Thursday. Also, the cold pool of\n850mb temperatures of -5 to -10C will trigger lake effects with\nthe relatively warm Lake Superior, which is running about 5 to \n10 C. We expect lake-effect precipitation will develop for the \nSouth Shore snowbelt region of northwest Wisconsin. While this \nwill be a rain and snow mixture during the day on Wednesday, \ncontinued cooling will turn the precipitation to mostly snow for\nWednesday night into Thursday. While moisture is generally \nlimited with this system, evaporation off the lake can \ncontribute a lot of moisture this time of year, and we may see \nprecipitation amounts higher than currently forecast, which may \nyield some light snowfall accumulations. \n\nFRIDAY \nA ridge of high pressure builds into the area for Friday, \nproducing quiet and dry weather which should persist into \nSaturday. Temperatures to warm back above normal by Saturday.",
